MammaprintTM: a comprehensive review
Mariana Brand  ̃ao1, Noam Pond  ́e1 & Martine Piccart-Gebhart*,1 1Institut Jules Bordet & L’Universite ́ Libre de Bruxelles (U.L.B.), 121, 1000, Brussels, Belgium *Author for correspondence: Tel.: +32 (0) 25413206; martine.piccart@bordet.be
The number of breast cancer (BC) cases is growing worldwide, being most frequently diagnosed in the early-setting. MammaprintTM is a 70-gene-expression signature, originally designed for selecting early BC patients with low risk of developing metastasis, so that they could be spared adjuvant chemotherapy. Its use as a prognostic biomarker has been extensively validated, both retrospectively and prospectively. However, its value as a predictive tool and as a clinically useful tool remains controversial. This review will describe how the test works, its application in the clinic and its limitations. Cost–effectiveness studies will be summarized. Finally, we will provide a perspective on the use of Mammaprint in the near future, as a valuable tool for personalizing the treatment of early BC patients.
First draft submitted: 19 March 2018; Accepted for publication: 1 August 2018; Published online: 29 August 2018
Keywords: adjuvant chemotherapy • breast neoplasms • gene-expression signatures
Breast cancer (BC) is diagnosed every year in approximately 1.2 million women worldwide, and is, in industrialized societies, most often localized (early BC) and curable [1]. Curative treatment of BC is based on surgical removal of the primary tumor, commonly followed by radiotherapy and, for tumors considered to be at high risk of relapse, by adjuvant chemotherapy [2]. Advances in adjuvant chemotherapy have significantly improved outcomes for women with early BC [3], but at the cost of important short- and long-term consequences for both patients and society [4,5]. Therefore, in the last 15 years, an intense debate has developed on the most appropriate means of defining which patients are at a sufficiently high risk of relapse to justify the use of chemotherapy [2]. Traditionally, a host of tumor and patient characteristics are taken into consideration to determine whether a patient is at high risk of relapse. These essentially phenotypical tumor characteristics are used to infer disease behavior. However, disease behavior is also driven by alterations occurring at the molecular level [6]. Advances in genome sequencing in the 2000s have led to multiple initiatives to go beyond disease phenotype into the genomic, transcriptomic and proteomic tumor landscapes, ultimately revealing the existence, under the overarching umbrella of BC, of multiple distinct pathologic entities [7]. This new molecular way to conceptualize BC, as well as the selfevident limitations of traditional risk assessment methods, gave birth to a number of attempts to create tools capable of providing a glimpse into disease biology to aid decision making – the gene-expression signatures (GES) [8]. Today, a number of GES measured on primary tumor tissue have been developed and are in current clinical use, holding the possibility of sparing a substantial proportion of patients not only from overtreatment but also from undertreatment. However, the expense related to performing GES and the absence, until recently, of prospective validation of their clinical utility have continued to fuel the debate surrounding their use. MammaprintTM was the first GES approved for use by the US FDA and was the first one to have its clinical utility validated prospectively as decision-making tool when results are discordant with classic criteria in a prospective randomized trial (MINDACT) [9,10]. This article will provide a thorough and critical review of Mammaprint, including its preclinical development, the studies which form the basis of its use in the clinic as well as how it compares to other available GES and its future prospects.
How was the signature developed?
Mammaprint was initially developed in archived fresh-frozen samples from a set of 78 young BC patients with tumor size less than 5 cm and negative lymph nodes (LN), not treated with adjuvant systemic therapy (Supplementary Table 1 & Figure 1). In this group, around half remained free of distant metastasis at five years [9]. The aim
