of the researchers was to develop a gene-signature that could differentiate the subgroup of patients with a low risk for distant metastasis from those experiencing metastasis in the first years after surgery. From the initial approximately 5000 genes tested by supervised analysis, a signature was created with 70 genes that best correlated with prognosis. After adjusting for classical prognostic factors, a multivariate model showed that the signature was a strong prognostic factor: patients with a ‘poor-prognosis signature’ had an odds ratio (OR) of 18 (95% CI: 3.3–94) of developing distant metastasis within five years compared with the ones with the ‘good-prognosis’ signature. The 70 genes included pertain predominantly to one of the six classical hallmarks of cancer (Figure 2) [6], largely covering proliferation, resistance to apoptosis, invasion and dissemination. Today, the signature is marketed by Agendia under the name of Mammaprint. The test was originally developed on fresh-frozen tissue, which hampered its practical use by oncologists as well as its validation on biobanked tumor samples of clinical trials with a long follow-up, but today it can be performed in formalin-fixed paraffin-embedded tissue, with equivalent analytical validity [35].
Historical perspective on the development of Mammaprint Early validation studies
Following initial development, most efforts consisted in retrospective validation series and pooled analysis, assessing the value of the gene-signature in different patient populations and settings [11–20,28–34] (Supplementary Table 1 & Figure 1). Patients who had neoadjuvant therapy were excluded from all these studies and as ‘distant metastasis-free survival’ (DMFS) was the end point used to develop the 70-gene signature, it remained the main end point of most studies. Taken together, the results of all these studies shed light not only on the potential of the test but also on important aspects of BC biology.
High prognostic value of Mammaprint for predicting distant relapse
It has long been recognized that BC has the ability to metastasize to distant sites even at an early point of tumor development. Even in small tumors (≤2 cm), Mammaprint can predict which patients have a higher risk of developing distant metastasis: 28% of ‘high-genomic risk’ patients by the gene-signature had a distant relapse at 10 years, compared with 13% of the ‘low-genomic risk’ patients (adjusted hazard ratio [HR]: 2.43; 95% CI: 1.6–3.8) [30]. This effect remained even in the very small pT1ab tumors (HR: 3.45; 95% CI: 1.0–11.5), thus further confirming the ability of Mammaprint to capture this intrinsic metastatic potential.
