LN status & value of Mammaprint
LN status [11] and, in particular, the number of positive LN [20] were not correlated with a gene-signature category, indicating that dissemination to distant sites (hematogenous spread) is distinct from dissemination to LN (lymphogenic spread). LN-positive patients (between one and three positive nodes) with a low-genomic risk had a lower risk of distant metastasis (DMFS of 98% at five years and 91% at ten years), compared with high-genomic risk patients (DMFS of 80% at 5 years and 76% at 10 years) [14]. These survival data are derived from patients treated between 1984 and 1996, of whom only 18% received endocrine treatment (ET) and none received chemotherapy; therefore, the DMFS rate of low-genomic risk patients was considered favorable, indicating that this patient subgroup could possibly also be spared chemotherapy [11,12,14]. However, in patients with four to nine positive LN [20], the low-genomic risk group had a DMFS at five years of 87% and at ten years of 67%, which do not justify withholding chemotherapy, demonstrating that tumor burden is still an important prognostic factor [14].
Combination with clinical risk indexes
Clinical risk indexes like the St. Gallen [36], Adjuvant! Online (AOL) [37,38] and others, are commonly used in clinical practice to predict DMFS or overall survival (OS) and, consequently, to select patients for adjuvant therapy. However, when adjusting these clinical risk indexes for the gene-signature, most of them did not retain an independent association with DMFS or OS [13], hinting that classic clinical risk factors are mainly a reflection of the intrinsic genetic properties of the tumor. Still, when combining clinical risk factors with the gene-signature in a multivariate model for the detection of distant metastasis, the area under the curve (AUC) value increased from 0.70 (95% CI: 0.61–0.79) to 0.76 (95% CI: 0.68–0.85) and the AUC for OS increased from 0.74 (95% CI: 0.66–0.82) to 0.82 (95% CI: 0.75–0.89) [13]. Furthermore, multiple studies have demonstrated that patients
