Postmenopausal patients
Is Mammaprint, initially developed in young BC patients, also useful in older, exclusively postmenopausal populations? In one series [16], only 27% of patients had a low-genomic risk, and 10-year DMFS was good both in lowand high-genomic risk groups: 100 versus 90%, respectively. However, in another series [17], 61% of patients had a low-genomic risk and it was demonstrated that Mammaprint was strongly prognostic; low-genomic risk patients had a 5-year DMFS of 93% compared with 72% in high-genomic risk patients (HR: 4.6; 95% CI: 1.8–12.0). This was further confirmed in pooled analyses in which more than half of the patients were postmenopausal [28,33,34].
Prognostic ability over time
In the updated analysis of the first validation series, after a median follow-up of 18.5 years, the HR for DMFS was 9.6 (95% CI: 4.2–22.1) in the first 5 years, but it was only 1.1 (95% CI: 0.5–2.5) in years 5–10 and 1.2 (95% CI: 0.2–6.0) in years 10–15 [12]. A similar trend was observed for OS. Therefore, the prognostic ability of Mammaprint may be reduced over time, being higher in the first five years after diagnosis and then declining. Nonetheless, the low number of long-term events could have contributed to these numbers. As the EBCTCG meta-analysis [3] has shown that the beneficial effect of chemotherapy mostly happens in the first five years after diagnosis, this does not diminish its value as an adjuvant treatment decision making tool. However, at least one study suggests that Mammaprint can identify an ultralow-risk population (score ≥0.355) with a breast cancer-specific survival (BCSS) rate between 94 and 97% at 20 years [21]. A meta-analysis showed that proliferation is the main component of the prognostic performance of Mammaprint and other GES [39] – in an analysis of the TransATAC study, GES based on cell proliferation showed a good performance for the prediction of late relapses, thus one can speculate that Mammaprint may have the same ability [40].
The RASTER trial
All of the aforementioned studies were retrospective and based on data from patients with different follow-up times, receiving a variable amount of adjuvant therapy, who are not representative of the modern BC patient populations, in which almost all women with estrogen receptor (ER)-positive tumors receive ET [41]. Therefore, a prospective study was still required to validate Mammaprint, but some logistical problems needed to be addressed first. The RASTER study [23] evaluated the feasibility of performing the gene-signature using fresh-frozen tissue in community hospitals in The Netherlands as well as how it changed physicians’ prescription of systemic therapy. Among evaluable patients, almost half had high-genomic risk, but only 43% had high-clinical risk according to the Dutch guidelines [42]. Interestingly, due to the fact that physicians’ prescription of systemic treatment was based on the Dutch guidelines, the use of Mammaprint actually resulted in an increase in the number of patients receiving chemotherapy and ET. Nevertheless, the RASTER study proved that Mammaprint is a feasible test to use in community hospitals [23]. The updated follow-up shows that among low-genomic risk patients, 5-year DMFS is identical whether or not AOL indicates a low- (94.3%) or a high-clinical risk (97.6%) [22].
The MINDACT trial
The MINDACT trial enrolled 6693 women with up to three positive LN, in nine European countries, with the goal of proving the clinical utility of adding Mammaprint to standard clinical–pathological criteria, for an improved selection of patients for adjuvant chemotherapy [10]. More specifically, the trial aimed to reduce the prescription of adjuvant chemotherapy by using the Mammaprint test, without impairing outcome. Modified AOL was used to determine high- and low-clinical risk (Figure 3) and patients were classified into four groups according to their clinical and genomic-risk: low-clinical/low-genomic, high-clinical/high-genomic, high-clinical/low-genomic and low-clinical/high-genomic. Randomization between chemotherapy versus no-chemotherapy only occurred in the discordant groups (high-clinical/low-genomic and low-clinical/high-genomic). The main end point was to prove
