ith low-genomic risk), corresponding to 23% of the total population. Finally, the authors assessed the rate of 5-year DMFS if only clinical-risk or genomic-risk scores would be applied to define which patients should receive chemotherapy and the results were strikingly similar: 95.0% with clinical-risk evaluation and 94.7% with genomic-risk assessment. Thus, the added value of Mammaprint is that with its use as an aid to treatment selection, the 5-year DMFS is maintained, but an additional 23% of the overall population will not receive chemotherapy, which may decrease toxicities and costs. Regarding 5-year disease-free survival (DFS) and OS, there were no differences between the chemotherapy versus no-chemotherapy groups within risk-discordant patients in the ITT population. However, in the per-protocol population, 5-year DFS was slightly worse in the high-clinical/low-genomic risk group without chemotherapy
