MINDACT design
Definition of the primary end point
A major criticism to the MINDACT trial is that the primary end point is based in a nonrandomized cohort of patients: high-clinical risk/low-genomic risk patients not receiving chemotherapy. Besides, a formal noninferiority design was not pursued, as it would lead to a much higher sample size, which was not feasible in terms of cost and time. However, treatment de-escalation in oncology would be impossible if we would always require a ‘non-inferiority’ design, due to its associated high costs and low interest from commercial companies to fund it. Therefore, other de-escalation studies have used this nonrandomized approach and their results were welcomed by both physicians and patients. An example is the single-arm APT trial, which tested the use of chemotherapy without anthracyclines plus trastuzumab for one year in 410 patients with small (≤2 cm) HER2-positive tumors [46]. Invasive DFS rate at only three years was 98.7% (95% CI: 97.6–99.8) and this leads to a practice-changing deescalation strategy in these patients, sparing them from the long-term effects of anthracyclines. Updated 7-year results confirmed the initial favorable prognosis [47]. Therefore, this regimen was endorsed by scientific guidelines, without the need for evaluating the potential harm associated with anthracyclines removal in a randomized fashion. Likewise, Mammaprint was also endorsed by scientific societies [48,49], even if the secondary end point shows that there might be a potential small gain in DMFS/DFS with the prescription of chemotherapy, as it was recognized that this small gain would be offset by the chemotherapy-associated side effects and risks.
Definition of clinical risk
The TRANSBIG consortium chose AOL to define clinical risk, as it was a validated, standardized assessment, freely available to any clinician and based on large clinical databases [50]. The TRANSBIG consortium, with the involvement of EUROPA DONNA, agreed that a 92% chance of survival at ten years without chemotherapy was a desirable goal, as any benefit from chemotherapy beyond this threshold would be of small magnitude and counterbalanced by its risks (namely premature menopause, cognitive dysfunction, cardiac damage and leukemia [4,51]). As adjuvant ET for women with ER-positive disease provides an estimated 4% survival gain at ten years, the threshold for this group was set at 88%. However, AOL did not include the levels of ER, PgR and Ki67 positivity to stratify Luminal A versus Luminal B tumors. Additionally, it might no longer be accessible [38]: however, the statisticians of MINDACT have provided a table that defines clinical risk as obtained with the use of AOL during the conduct of MINDACT [10].
Definition of the DMFS lower boundary
In noninferiority trials, the definition of a threshold for what is considered ‘not clinically significant’ is always a matter of subjectivity and debate. Therefore, when the MINDACT trial was designed, a large discussion was set within the TRBANSBIG consortium to define the 5-year DMFS lower boundary for MINDACT [52]. As explained above, it was decided that a 5-year DMFS ≥92% would be considered acceptable. However, this definition should be regarded as a trade-off in terms of the proportion of patients who have a distant relapse versus those who are safely spared from chemotherapy. Therefore, this compromise cannot be considered as an ‘applies-to-all’ decision and should be explained to the patient before ordering Mammaprint.
Potential chemotherapy benefit in 5-year survival rates
The MINDACT trial was not powered to show differences in survival according to the prescription of chemotherapy. However, it is true that in the high-clinical risk/low-genomic risk ITT and per-protocol populations, the HR for 5year DMFS, OS and DFS was always below 1.0, favoring chemotherapy, including in ER-positive/HER2-negative LN-negative patients (Table 1). A reason may be that 8% of these patients had HER2-positive tumors and, probably, they were undertreated with ET alone. Another explanation for this possible positive effect of chemotherapy in lowproliferative tumors may reside in its indirect endocrine effects: 35% of the high-clinical/low-genomic risk patients were ≤50 years, likely being premenopausal, and 98% were hormone receptor-positive. Thus, chemotherapy
