Long-term survival
The recently published European Network for Health Technology Assessment report expresses the concern that the possible decrease in DMFS at five years could widen in the next five years and result in a substantial harm in terms of 10-year OS [55]. However, this increase in distant recurrences in the no-chemotherapy arm versus the chemotherapy arm seems highly unlikely: the EBCTCG meta-analysis involving 100,000 patients showed that the effect of chemotherapy is mainly observed during the first five years after treatment and that after this initial period the survival curves do not further separate [3]. Thus, it seems fair to assume that 10-year results from MINDACT will show similar rates of recurrence events in both trial arms. Moreover, it is not possible to statistically ‘model’ a prediction for the 10-year OS, as the population included in this trial is a new highly selected population defined by a diagnostic test not available in the past. Additionally, in the overall population, DFS events corresponded mostly to second primary cancers (42%), followed by distant metastases (36%), locoregional recurrences (16%) or deaths due to any cause (6%). As most second primary cancers and locoregional recurrences can still be treated with curative intent, it is unlikely that substantial harm in OS will emerge in the no-chemotherapy arm.
Quality of life
Earlier studies stated that the reduction on the rate of prescription of chemotherapy among the high-clinical/lowgenomic risk group could translate into reduced toxicity and thus better quality of life (QoL), but no formal evaluation of QoL was performed in the MINDACT trial [10]. The reason is that it was perceived by both authors and patient advocates that it would be too burdensome for the patients and additionally, at the time, there was no validated QoL instrument [56]. While the utility of using Mammaprint in terms of QoL cannot be stated, common clinical sense would indicate that for the 23% of patients who are spared the side effects of chemotherapy, there is a QoL benefit extending over a few months or even a few years in case of severe cognitive dysfunction, sleep disturbances and fatigue [51,57,58].
Predictive ability
Kok et al. [27] evaluated the ability of Mammaprint to predict which patients benefit the most from adjuvant treatment with tamoxifen. However, in these ER-positive patients receiving adjuvant tamoxifen, being ‘incompleteendocrine responsive’ (either ER or progesterone receptor [PgR] between 0 and 49%, with at least ER >10%) was more predictive of a worse BCSS (HR: 7.22; 95% CI: 2.17–24.0) than having a high-genomic risk (HR; 2.78; 95% CI: 1.3–5.9). After adjustment for ER/PgR positivity and other clinical factors, Mammaprint was no longer significantly associated with BCSS (HR: 1.8; 95% CI: 0.77–4.61). The predictive ability of Mammaprint for the benefit of adjuvant chemotherapy has not been prospectively demonstrated, as the MINDACT trial was not powered to directly compare outcomes between chemotherapy versus no-chemotherapy in the clinical/genomic-risk discordant groups [10]. This is a limitation, as the greatest utility of a genomic testing is to guide decisions regarding the use of chemotherapy and, for that, it should be able to predict which patients will benefit from chemotherapy or not. This means that, for now, Mammaprint’s ability to select patients for chemotherapy is based solely on its prognostic capabilities.
Use in HER2-positive or TNBC patients
In a retrospective pooled analysis in HER2-positive disease, among 89 patients, all chemotherapy and trastuzumabnaive, 22% were classified as low-genomic risk, which is an intriguing observation as HER2-positive BC is considered an aggressive disease [29]. Low-genomic risk patients had a 10-year DMFS of 84% in the absence of chemotherapy
