and trastuzumab, while high-genomic risk patients had a worse outcome (10-year DMFS of 55%). However, in the MINDACT trial, HER2-positive patients constituted only 8% of the high-clinical/low-genomic risk population (n = 124) and a separate analysis was not performed in these patients. Thus, the value of Mammaprint in this population is uncertain [10]. Likewise, the usefulness of the gene-signature is probably very low or absent in triple-negative patients, as 90–96% of them were classified as high-genomic risk in the MINDACT trial [10] and in earlier validation series [20,31,33]. This is in agreement with data from a large meta-analysis of GES, in which most of the HER2-positive and triple-negative tumors were classified as high-risk, therefore limiting its clinical utility in these patients. Another comprehensive meta-analysis using both clinicopathologic and gene-expression data and focusing on the main molecular subtypes showed that among ER-positive/HER2-negative tumors, proliferation-related genes were indeed the strongest prognostic parameter; however, in the triple-negative subset, the most important prognostic genes were the ones related with immune response and in the HER2-positive group, tumor invasion-related genes were the more relevant ones [59].
Use of Mammaprint in clinical trials
Mammaprint is being used in clinical trials, most notably in the I-SPY2 neoadjuvant trial (NCT01042379). Here, trial population is categorized into eight subtypes defined by three biomarkers: hormone receptors (ER/PgR), HER2 and Mammaprint. The gene-signature was chosen as a biomarker, as it was previously shown that a high-genomic risk was associated with higher neoadjuvant chemosensitivity [24]. For this trial, patients traditionally classified as high-genomic risk by Mammaprint were further divided in two categories: high-risk1 (MP1) and (ultra)high-risk2 (MP2) based in a higher cut-point [44]. The MINT I study also demonstrated that patients classified as MP2 had a greater sensitivity to neoadjuvant chemotherapy, as their pCR rate was 44% compared with a pCR rate of 24% in MP1 patients [45].
Practical application in the clinic
Mammaprint was the first GES approved by the FDA, in 2007. Following the publication of MINDACT, both the American Society of Clinical Oncology and the European Group on Tumour Markers published updated guidelines recommending the use of Mammaprint [48,49]. The National Comprehensive Cancer Network still favors the use of Oncotype DX, though it recognizes MammaprintTM as a valid alternative [41]. Consensus opinion is, today, that a low-genomic risk Mammaprint result can be used in patients with ER-positive/HER2-negative early BC, with up to three positives nodes, considered as having a high-clinical risk of relapse according to traditional criteria, to safely forego the use of adjuvant chemotherapy. Additionally, Mammaprint was incorporated in the 8th TNM edition for the classification of BC, along other GES, as a way of downstaging selected ER-positive/HER2-negative LN-negative tumors with low-genomic risk into the same prognostic category as T1a-T1bN0M0 [60]. It should, nonetheless, be discussed with the patient that there might be a small loss in DMFS and DFS with the avoidance of chemotherapy in this setting. A practical approach to the application of Mammaprint in the clinic for patients with ER-positive/HER2-negative tumors is provided in Figure 4. As Mammaprint’s prognostic performance is driven by genes related to proliferation [39], an open question is if Mammaprint can be replaced by a simpler and cheaper method of evaluating cell proliferation, like nuclear grade or the Ki67 score. A study tested the Genomic Grade (a GES) in 883 tumor samples of patients enrolled in the BIG 1–98 trial and showed that among the 60% of patients who were centrally classified as nuclear grade 2, the ones classified as GG1 (low proliferative) by the Genomic Grade had a 10-year DMFS of 99%, compared with 85% for patients with GG3 (high proliferative) [61]. Thus, this molecular assessment showed to accurately define prognosis in this large proportion of patients with an intermediate nuclear grade. Regarding Ki67 positivity, it is a proven prognostic factor both in LN-negative and LN-positive early BC patients, being associated with worse DFS and OS [62]. However, its assessment is still highly variable among pathologists and no standard cut-point has been defined, making it application in clinic troublesome [63]. This was recognized by the American Society of Clinical Oncology that does not endorse the use of Ki67 for adjuvant treatment decision [49]. Even so, some guidelines state that Ki67 may be used to distinguish between Luminal A-like and Luminal B-like subtypes and recommend the use of adjuvant chemotherapy in patients with ER-positive tumors and a high Ki67 [64,65]. However, a MINDACT substudy comparing the classification of tumors by a central pathology lab using modern immunohistochemistry according to the 2013 St Gallen guidelines [66] to a molecular type classification based on Mammaprint and BluePrintTM showed provocative results [67]. In this analysis of 5806 tumor samples, 34% of tumors were classified
