company is currently working to decentralize the test, creating a limited number of accredited labs across Europe. There is also an added cost to the regular pathological assessment, as the performance of the test costs, by itself, 2675€ in France [69], 3000€ in Germany [70], £2207 in the UK [71] and US$4200 in the USA [72]. Still, its utility in clinical practice was demonstrated in a pooled analysis in which the rate of prescription of chemotherapy among 790 patients decreased from 52 to 43% after performing Mammaprint [73]. Likewise, in an analysis involving more than 2000 patients, the use of Mammaprint was associated with a 10% decrease in the use of chemotherapy [74]. In the prospective WSG PRIME study [75], however, among 452 patients, there was a net increase in the use of chemotherapy of 1.9%, due to the fact that patients with low-clinical risk who were initially advised not to receive chemotherapy, were then advised to receive chemotherapy based on a high-genomic risk result, which is a strategy not proven to be beneficial in the MINDACT trial. Therefore, if this study was conducted today, probably these patients would not receive chemotherapy and the use of Mammaprint would then be associated with a reduction of 13% in the chemotherapy prescription rate. Currently, the IMPACt prospective registry (NCT02670577) is assessing the influence of Mammaprint on chemotherapy plus ET versus ET alone treatment decisions in 481 ER-positive/HER2-negative patients. Analyses have usually shown that Mammaprint is a cost-effective test [73,76]. In the UK, the use of Mammaprint compared with standard care leads to a gain of 0.18 quality-adjusted life years (QALYs), at a cost of £1097/QALY gained [71]. In an US analysis, Oncotype DX and Mammaprint were directly compared and the results showed that Mammaprint is the most cost-effective test [77]. Another US analysis compared Mammaprint to AOL: its use spared 10% of patients from chemotherapy, with an increase of 0.15 QALYs, at a cost of US$700 to US$10,000/QALY, depending on the model used, but being cost-effective in every scenario [78]. In Spain, the use of Mammaprint compared with AOL or Oncotype DX leads to a gain in QALYs between 0.23 and 0.75, with a cost of €1457/QALY gained [79]. In contrast, a French analysis compared Mammaprint with AOL and with a chemotherapy-for-all strategy and concluded that the use of AOL would be cost-effective, but the use of Mammaprint or of chemotherapy-for-all would not [69]. However, it is important to mention that certain specificities of the French healthcare system and practice reduce the relevance of these results for other countries [72,80]. These analyses were mostly based on retrospective series and applied Mammaprint to a nonselected population. This ‘blind’ approach spares chemotherapy in about only a fifth of patients, corresponding to the high-clinical risk/low-genomic risk population. However, if these analyses applied the stepwise procedure recommended in the MINDACT trial of first assessing clinical risk and then ordering Mammaprint only to the high-clinical risk patients, the test would be requested to just half of the potential patients and would spare chemotherapy in half of these tested patients (the ones classified as high-clinical/low-genomic risk). Therefore, this stepwise approach would probably even further improve Mammaprint’s cost–effectiveness. By contrast, in TAILORx, Oncotype DX was applied to every patient who had an indication to chemotherapy according to the National Comprehensive Cancer Network guidelines. Even after this preselection, 70% of the patients presented a low-clinical risk according to the MINDACT definition and, thus, could have been spared chemotherapy even before being tested with Oncotype DX; this suggests that for a majority of patients’ traditional criteria can still determine management in the adjuvant setting [54].
Comparison with other GES
A large number of additional GES are currently available for use in clinical practice. These GES used different methods for assessing gene expression, as well as different sets of genes, generating the possibility of different performances. Though currently no data points toward better clinical outcomes for patients with one particular GES over another, several studies have compared the classification of patients according to risk by different GES with Mammaprint, when applied to the same set of samples. Table 3 summarizes the principal characteristics of the most widely used GES. An early attempt to compare GES was made by Fan et al., who tested five different GES (PAM50, Mammaprint, Oncotype DX, the 2-gene ratio component of the Breast Cancer Index and wound response) on 295 samples. Results show all tests except the 2-gene ratio predicted outcomes similarly, with a high correlation of results between patients, with concordance between Oncotype DX and Mammaprint being as high as 81% [81]. However, this was an in silico comparison and it was not based on the commercial GES assays, which could in part explain why these results contrast with more recent studies such as the larger OPTIMA prelim trial. In OPTIMA prelim, 313 patients with ER-positive/HER2-negative early BC with tumors ≥3 cm and/or 1–9 positive LN were randomized between standard (chemotherapy + ET) or test-directed treatment (chemotherapy
