Future perspective
Over the last 15 years, Mammaprint has proved to be a powerful prognostic tool for early BC patients, especially for ER-positive/HER2-negative with up to three positive LN. However, the first validation series showed that its ability for predicting relapse may drop after the first five years from diagnosis [12], leading to the question if this is an useful tool for the selection of patients to adjuvant treatments other than chemotherapy. Indeed, as we are still waiting for the long-term data from the MINDACT trial, an important open research question is if Mammaprint can be used for selecting the best duration of adjuvant ET. A positive hint came from the retrospective analysis of a Swedish study, in which patients with ultralow-risk tumors as defined by Mammaprint had an extremely good survival even without adjuvant ET [21]. If these findings are validated in MINDACT, this could lead to the use of Mammaprint to select patients for extended adjuvant ET. Earlier validation studies included patients with all types of BC. However, our understanding of BC biology has evolved significantly over these past years and it is now known that the different subtypes of BC have very distinct biological and clinical behaviors [7]. Indeed, the above-mentioned meta-analysis [39,59] have shown that GES mostly based on proliferation markers are probably not useful in the triple-negative or HER2-positive group of patients as they usually have high rates of proliferation. Therefore, Mammaprint as a predictive tool for adjuvant systematic treatment should be probably used only for the ER-positive/HER2-negative subgroup of patients with high-clinical risk, as it was demonstrated in the MINDACT trial that patients with tumors with low-proliferation markers (nuclear grade 1) are usually low-clinical risk and therefore do not benefit from the use of Mammaprint [10]. On the other hand, for the neoadjuvant setting, Mammaprint is the only GES that has some data suggesting a role as predictor of benefit with neoadjuvant treatment, including in high-proliferating tumors, both with chemotherapy [24–26,45] and with the poly(ADP-ribose) polymerase (PARP) inhibitor veliparib [44], especially when a higher cut-point is used to separate high-risk from ultrahigh-risk patients. So, in the future, it is expected that Mammaprint will be increasingly used as a selection/stratifying factor in neoadjuvant trials with ER-positive/HER2negative and/or triple-negative patients; it may also be combined with other biomarkers, like DNA-repair deficiency biomarkers, to increase its predictive ability [87]. The IMPAKT Working Group 2012 recommended the creation of registries for patients who are subjected to genomic testing in the daily clinical practice [88]. An example is the observational ‘NBRST’ registry (NCT01479101), including patients receiving neoadjuvant treatment after having been tested by BluePrintTM/Mammaprint. It confirmed that tumors with a high-genomic risk were more chemosensitive than low-genomic risk tumors in a real-world setting [26]. However, similar registries would also be useful in other regions of the world, like Asia, in which the prognostic and clinical utility of Mammaprint has not been proven yet. Meanwhile, new genomic profiles using DNA microarray platforms or whole genome arrays will probably be developed to explore other markers that could lead to different types of prediction (of response to treatment or of early or late relapse) in a single assay, which could be added to the Mammaprint genomic-risk [89]. Another option would be to combine a baseline tissue-based Mammaprint genomic-risk with the analysis of baseline and postneoadjuvant treatment circulating tumor-DNA to identify patients at higher risk of early relapse [90]. In conclusion, the clinical utility of Mammaprint in early BC patients has been prospectively validated by the MINDACT trial, which showed that patients with a high-clinical risk but with a low-genomic risk could be safely spared chemotherapy. Long-term survival data from MINDACT are awaited. Nevertheless, in the era of precision medicine, Mammaprint has already proven to be a useful tool for the tailoring of adjuvant chemotherapy, potentially sparing up to one in five patients from chemotherapy, with an expected decrease in toxicity and improvement of QoL.
