Executive summary
• How was the gene-expression signature (GES) developed?
• GES were developed to define which early breast cancer patients benefit from the use of adjuvant chemotherapy, as it leads to important short- and long-term consequences for both patient and society. • MammaprintTM was developed in 2002 as a 70-gene signature with the ability of distinguishing patients with high risk of distant metastasis at 5 years (‘high-genomic risk’) from those with a low risk (‘low-genomic risk’).
• Problems and controversies regarding the test and the MINDACT trial. • The MINDACT trial was not powered to directly compare outcomes between patients receiving chemotherapy versus no-chemotherapy, therefore the predictive ability of Mammaprint for selecting patients who would benefit the most from chemotherapy could not be proven. • Its prognostic ability in the adjuvant setting for HER-positive and triple-negative patients has been questioned, as most of these patients are classified as ‘high-genomic risk’.
• Practical application in the clinic.
• Mammaprint is the first GES to have its clinical utility validated in a prospective randomized trial (the MINDACT trial) as a decision-making tool when genomic-risk results are discordant with clinical criteria. • Mammaprint is approved by many health authorities throughout the world, based on the results of extensive studies on its analytical validity, clinical validity, and more recently, clinical utility in a prospective setting (the MINDACT trial). • Mammaprint was incorporated in the 8th TNM edition for the classification of breast cancer, along other GES, as a way of downstaging selected estrogen receptor-positive/HER2-negative node-negative tumors with low-genomic risk into the same prognostic category as T1a-T1bN0M0.
• Use of Mammaprint in clinical trials. • In the neoadjuvant setting, a higher cut-point separates patients with high-risk from ultrahigh-risk, which predicts the sensitivity to chemotherapy, even in high proliferation tumors like HER2-positive and triple-negative and lead to the use of Mammaprint as a stratification factor in the neoadjuvant I-SPY2 trial.
• Cost–effectiveness of Mammaprint. • Cost–effectiveness analyses have mostly shown that Mammaprint is likely to be a cost-effective test.
• Precision medicine.
• In the era of precision medicine, Mammaprint has already proven to be an important tool for the tailoring of adjuvant chemotherapy, potentially sparing an important proportion of patients from aggressive treatments and leading to a reduction in toxicities and costs, with an expected improvement in the quality of life of early breast cancer patients.
